{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs36 Graphing Guidelines\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0  Prof.\u160 ?Jack Reilly\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0  F2026\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Every graph in your report must follow these guidelines.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Graph type}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Default to a {\b bar graph}. Do not use another type of graph (for example, a pie chart) without talking to your UCA or Professor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab If there are 5 or fewer bars, arrange the bars vertically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab If there are 6 or more bars, arrange the bars horizontally.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Color}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Everything is black \u8211- no gray!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Be careful: Excel will sometimes default to a very dark gray.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Bars are solid black.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Axis lines are solid black.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab No borders on bars, and no extra shading on bars.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab No border on your graph.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Text}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ALL text is Times New Roman 12pt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Double check your title is 12pt! When you update everything to TNR 12pt, sometimes the title will default back to a larger font. You may have to adjust the font size of your title a second time.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Axis titles should be {\b bold}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The title should be {\b bold}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab \u8220"n=sample size\u8221" should be under your title.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Axes and data labels}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The y-axis starts at 0.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Use outside-end data labels. You will know this is done correctly if percentages display above your bars.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Use major outside tick marks on your axis.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b Important}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 Copy and paste your graph into the report. {\b No screenshots!}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Example of a graph that follows the guidelines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b About this example}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 This example uses {\b sample data}. Skills Win Coaching Organization is a real Syracuse University program, but the numbers shown are invented for teaching purposes and are not actual SWCO results.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1268\pich847\picwgoal9175\pichgoal6129 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Check it against the list above: bar graph, three bars so arranged vertically, solid black bars and axis lines, Times New Roman 12pt throughout, bold title and bold axis titles, y-axis starting at 0, outside-end data labels showing percentages, \u8220"n=\u8221" under the title, major outside tick marks, and no border.\par}
